\details{
DHARMa residuals are created by simulating from the fitted model, and comparing the simulated values to the observed data. It can occur that all simulated values are higher or smaller than the observed data, in which case they get a residual < 1/(nSim + 1) or > nSim/(nSim + 1) (exactly 0 or 1 for method = "traditional" or refit = TRUE, see \link{getQuantile}). I refer to these values as simulation outliers, or simply outliers. For discrete responses, observations that are equal to the smallest or largest simulated values can also fall into these outer cells (with probability 1/(t + 1), with t the number of tied simulations), so that the outlier frequency under H0 is the same as for continuous responses.

Because no data was simulated in the range of the observed values, we don't know "how strongly" these values deviate from the model expectation, so the term "outlier" should be used with a grain of salt. It is not a judgment about the magnitude of the residual deviation, but simply a dichotomous sign that we are outside the simulated range. Moreover, the number of outliers will decrease as we increase the number of simulations.

To test if the outliers are a concern, testOutliers implements 2 options (bootstrap, binomial), which can be chosen via the parameter "type". The third option (default) chooses bootstrap for residuals with method = "traditional" (incl. refit = TRUE) with duplicated non-integer simulated values and nObs <= 500, and else binomial. For residuals recalculated with \link{recalculateResiduals}, nObs is the number of recalculated residuals.

The binomial test considers that under the null hypothesis that the model is correct (i.e. observations and simulations come from the same distribution), the probability that a given observation is higher (lower) than all simulations is 1/(nSim + 1), so that the number of outliers is binomially distributed. This expectation is exact for continuous responses, for PIT residuals (also of discrete responses, because ties are broken at random, see \link{getQuantile}), and for integer-valued responses with method = "traditional" (because of the randomization of integer values). It does not hold for method = "traditional" with discrete non-integer responses (duplicate non-integer values in the simulations), because observations tied with the smallest or largest simulations are not counted as outliers, so that the binomial test finds too few outliers. For fitted models (and refit = TRUE), the expectation is approximate, and the test is usually slightly conservative.

For this reason, the testOutlier function implements an alternative procedure that uses the bootstrap to generate a simulation-based expectation for the outliers: each of nBoot simulations is treated as observed data and compared with the remaining nSim - 1 simulations. Because the null distribution is estimated from the simulations, the bootstrap test rejects slightly too often for small nSim (e.g. in up to 10\% instead of 5\% of the cases under H0 with nSim = 50 and nBoot = 50), so it should be used with reasonably high values of nSim and nBoot (I recommend at least 1000 for both). Because of the high runtime, type = "default" does not use it when nObs > 500.

Both binomial and bootstrap generate a null expectation, and then test for an excess or lack of outliers. Per default, testOutliers() looks for both, so if you get a significant p-value, you have to check if you have too many or too few outliers. An excess of outliers is to be interpreted as too many values outside the simulation envelope. This could be caused by overdispersion, or by what we classically call outliers. A lack of outliers would be caused, for example, by underdispersion.

Note that for type = "bootstrap", the reported confidence interval is the 2.5\% - 97.5\% quantile range of the outlier frequencies simulated under H0, not a confidence interval for the observed outlier frequency.
